__notes__.note.rungs.setting-exact-part-what-tower:
\begin{quote}
\emph{Hypothesis (R), the rung reading.} The internal state space of an $n$-twistor composite is the spinor module of the $n$-th rung of the tower above $\Cl(2,4)$, $\Lambda^{\bullet}(W \oplus R_{n})$, on which the transported algebra acts as in Fact \claimref{rungs.imported-tower-one-qubit-per-rung}: each twistor of the composite occupies one new rung, and the constituents are the states with one rung occupied.
\end{quote}

__notes__.note.tower.setting-single-step-recalled:
Throughout, $\Cl(p,q)$ is the real Clifford algebra of the nondegenerate form of signature $(p,q)$ on $\R^n$, $n = p + q$, with mixed signature $p, q \geq 1$ where null vectors are in question; notation follows \cite{ButlerT1}. Rule 1, the Witt completion, adjoins a hyperbolic plane $H = \operatorname{span}\{e, f\}$ with $e^2 = 1$, $f^2 = -1$, $e \cdot f = 0$, equivalently a null pair $v = \tfrac12(e+f)$, $w = \tfrac12(e-f)$ with $v^2 = w^2 = 0$ and $v \cdot w = \tfrac12$. The ladder pair $(v, w)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
vw + wv = 1, \qquad v^2 = w^2 = 0,
\end{equation}
and generates, with the base, the completed algebra $\Cl(p{+}1, q{+}1)$. The single step is imported once, with its matrix-unit labelling fixed for the whole paper:

odd-extensions.belongs-tower.formal:
Under the dark-matter paper's Theorem 3.5 the residue of a sector is the signature of the soldered vector space, the image of the tangent space in the algebra with $v^{2} = g(v,v)$ (Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-copy-theorem-soldered-residue}). For the algebra $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ of Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.are-ascents-pair-signed}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the soldered vector space is the Lorentz frame $\{I,V\}^{\perp} = e(TM)$, the image of the soldering form, of signature $(1,3)$ for every $\Lambda$ (Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.are-ascents-pair-signed}(iv)); $V$ is not in it, being orthogonal to $e(TM)$ with $\nabla V = e$ (Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-frozen-point});
\item[(ii)] hence $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ has tower charge $6$: it is a sub-object of this world's rung, the intermediate algebra of this world's own ascent, and not the ground shadow of the residue-$5$ or residue-$7$ tower whose abstract algebra it shares;
\item[(iii)] the coincidence $\Cl(I^{\perp}) \cong \Cl(1,4)$ for $\Lambda > 0$ is induced by an isometry of quadratic spaces ($I^{\perp}$ has signature $(1,4)$ outright) but by no map of soldered data: the isometry carries $e_{V}$, which is not soldered, to a fifth tangent direction of the five-dimensional shadow, which is, and carries the four-dimensional soldered space into a proper subspace of that shadow's tangent space.
\end{enumerate}

odd-extensions.mirror-inside-complexifie.formal:
In $\Cl(2,3)$, the time extension of this world with $e_{5}^{2} = +1$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the generators $e_{1}, \ldots, e_{4}$ generate a copy of $\Cl(1,3)$, this world's shadow;
\item[(ii)] the bivectors $e_{\mu}e_{5}$, $\mu = 1, \ldots, 4$, anticommute pairwise, square to $(-1, +1, +1, +1)$, and generate the whole even subalgebra, which is a copy of $\Cl(3,1)$, the mirror's shadow;
\item[(iii)] the two copies meet exactly in the even part of the shadow, $\Cl^{0}(1,3) \cong \Mat(2,\C)$ (its complex unit the shadow's pseudoscalar $e_{1}e_{2}e_{3}e_{4}$, central in the even part), of dimension eight, the Lorentz algebra with its Weyl spinors; and together with $\omega$ they span everything, $\Cl(2,3) = \Cl(1,3) \oplus \omega\,\Cl(1,3)$;
\item[(iv)] no automorphism of $\Cl(2,3)$ carries the copy of $\Cl(1,3)$ onto the copy of $\Cl(3,1)$; the natural map between their generating sets is multiplication by the central $\omega$, $e_{\mu} \mapsto \omega e_{\mu}$, which squares to $-e_{\mu}^{2}$ (the Wick rotation) and is complex-linear for the central unit $\omega = \pm I_{5}$, a different unit from that of (iii) and not identified with it, not an automorphism of the real algebra.
\end{enumerate}
In $\Cl(1,4)$, the space extension with $e_{5}^{2} = -1$: the generators $e_{\mu}$ and the bivectors $e_{\mu}e_{5}$ both generate copies of $\Cl(1,3)$, the elements $\omega e_{\mu}$ generate a third, and $\Cl(3,1)$ admits no unital embedding into $\Cl(1,4)$ at all. The same statements hold for the mirror's two extensions with the roles of $\Cl(1,3)$ and $\Cl(3,1)$ exchanged.

odd-extensions.two-towers-one-curved-uni.formal:
Both oriented towers are soldered to the one gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), the mirror reading its derived metric with the sign reversed, $-g$, so the mirror's tractor bundle has signature $(4,2)$ (this world's tractors being the vectors of $\Cl(2,4)$ carried by the tractor gauge field of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection}, the mirror's those of $\Cl(4,2)$) and its frozen point has norm $-Q(I)$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] in a de Sitter universe the intermediate algebra of this world's ascent is the doubled $\Cl(1,4)$ and the mirror's is the complexified $\Cl(4,1)$; in an anti-de Sitter universe they are the complexified $\Cl(2,3)$ and the doubled $\Cl(3,2)$. The rule is: doubled if and only if (de Sitter and this world) or (anti-de Sitter and mirror);
\item[(ii)] the even subalgebras $\Cl^{0}(1,4)$ and $\Cl^{0}(4,1)$ have the same presentation, the bivectors $e_{\mu}e_{5}$ anticommuting with squares $(+,-,-,-)$, so the two towers' conformal-to-de-Sitter spin groups, spinor modules and MacDowell-Mansouri bivector algebras coincide; the derived content of the dark-matter paper's copy theorem, which runs through the spin group, does not see the doubling;
\item[(iii)] what differs is the bare fifth generator alone: $e_{5}^{2} = -1$ with $e_{5} = \pm\omega'$ on the two summands in the doubled case, against $e_{5}^{2} = +1$ with $e_{5} = \omega'^{-1}\omega$, a central complex unit times the pseudoscalar, in the complexified case.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the sign of $\Lambda$ does not select the visible tower: it distinguishes the two towers' intermediate algebras, but through an element no derived quantity of the series uses, and that element is the unit vector along $V$, which Proposition \claimref{odd-extensions.belongs-tower} places outside the soldered space and Fact \claimref{dark-energy.point-infinity-has-no-physical-fluctuation} places in the pure-gauge sector.
